cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this analysis or its method, please cite it as below."
title: "The ten-degree rule is an undeclared Arrhenius model"
abstract: >-
  An audit of the electrolytic-capacitor industry's standard life-derating shortcut
  (life doubles every 10 C the operating temperature drops below rated). Solving the
  rule for the activation energy it implies shows the rule is itself an Arrhenius
  model, with an Ea that varies by 77% depending on where on the temperature scale
  it is applied, and that produces optimistic life predictions -- by nearly 4x in the
  case examined -- at activation energies plausible for electrolyte-evaporation wear-out.
  Every number is reproduced from first principles by verify.py, with no proprietary
  or measured data.
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: "de Paula"
    given-names: "Marcos"
repository-code: "https://github.com/marcospaula/ten-degree-rule"
url: "https://github.com/marcospaula/ten-degree-rule"
license: MIT
date-released: 2026-08-16
keywords:
  - reliability engineering
  - accelerated life testing
  - Arrhenius equation
  - activation energy
  - electrolytic capacitors
  - reproducible analysis
references:
  - type: report
    title: "Aluminum Electrolytic Capacitor - Technical Notes"
    authors:
      - name: "Rubycon Corporation"
    notes: >-
      Document 3925-1e, section 5-1. States the ten-degree rule as Eq. 10 and
      confirms it deviates from a true Arrhenius model outside the 70-90 C
      window, which is the primary-source validation for this analysis. No
      numeric Ea is published in this document. See references.md.
    url: "https://www.rubycon.co.jp/wp-content/uploads/products-aluminum/al-technical-note_en.pdf"
  - type: report
    title: >-
      General Descriptions of Aluminum Electrolytic Capacitors / Application
      Guidelines for Aluminum Electrolytic Capacitors
    authors:
      - name: "Nichicon Corporation"
    notes: >-
      Technical Notes CAT.8101H, section 2-9-3. Names the rule "Arrhenius
      theory" and states its applicability floor at a minimum of 40 C -- the
      same temperature used in this analysis' worked example. No numeric Ea
      is published in this document. See references.md.
    url: "https://www.nichicon.co.jp/english/products/pdf/aluminum.pdf"
  - type: generic
    title: "Ten-degree rule (2x/10C rule) for electrolytic capacitor life"
    notes: "Industry-standard shortcut, published in long-life capacitor datasheets (Nichicon, Rubycon, Panasonic)."
